nk removal is accepted, then increase N = N*2 (finer), but not exceeding the length of the current list. Stop when N >= length (i.e., singleton granularity? Actually when N is >= length, each chunk is at most 1 item? Wait, if N chunks over L items, chunk size is roughly L/N. When N >= L, each chunk is 0 or 1 item. That would be singleton granularity meaning each item is its own chunk. That matches the typical ddmin termination: test granularity up to the list length. So I'll go with granularity = number of chunks.\n\nBut the spec says \"Start chunk removal at granularity two.\" That is number of chunks = 2. \"Slightly coarser granularity\" would be fewer chunks, e.g., N-1. \"Double granularity\" would be N*2. \"Stop at singleton granularity\" would be when N reaches 1 (one chunk) or when N equals the list length (each chunk is singleton). Actually if N=1, that's a single chunk, which is coarse. If we stop at that, we wouldn't test finer granularities. But typical ddmin stops when you can't reduce further at any granularity, meaning you test all granularities from 2 up to L. So I think \"singleton granularity\" means each chunk is a singleton (size 1). So we stop when N >= L (i.e., granularity is at least as fine as each item individual). Then we perform a final check to ensure 1-minimality by testing each item individually? The spec says \"Return the reduced items, attempt count, and an explicit one-minimal check.\" So the function should also verify that the final reduced set is 1-minimal (i.e., removing any single item would fail the condition). That can be done by checking each item individually.\n\nThus I'll implement:\n- Input case has \"items\" list and \"required_failure_items\" set.\n- Define a helper function `interesting(subset)` that returns True if all required_failure_items are present in subset.\n- Start with current_list = items.\n- attempt_count = 0.\n- Define granularity as number of chunks, start N=2.\n- While True:\n    - If N > len(current_list):