se standard library, no randomness.\n\nI'll implement accordingly.\n\nLet's code.\n\nPotential edge cases: If required_failure_items is empty? Possibly not, but handle gracefully.\n\nI'll produce Python source code as a string.\n\nI need to output exactly one JSON object with \"implementation\" key.\n\nI'll write the Python source in a triple-quoted string, then escape for JSON.\n\nLet me draft the code:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    items = case[\"items\"]\n    required = set(case[\"required_failure_items\"])\n    # Helper\n    def interesting(lst):\n        return all(item in lst for item in required)\n    # start\n    current = items[:]  # copy\n    attempts = 0\n    N = 2  # number of chunks\n    while True:\n        L = len(current)\n        if N > L:\n            N = L\n        if N <= 0:\n            break\n        # compute chunk sizes\n        base = L // N\n        rem = L % N\n        chunk_sizes = [base + 1] * rem + [base] * (N - rem)  # first chunks larger\n        # since we iterate left to right, use cumulative index\n        start = 0\n        accepted = False\n        for i in range(N):\n            size = chunk_sizes[i]\n            end = start + size\n            candidate = current[:start] + current[end:]\n            attempts += 1\n            if interesting(candidate):\n                current = candidate\n                # restart with coarser granularity (fewer chunks)\n                N = max(1, N - 1)\n                accepted = True\n                break\n            start = end\n        if not accepted:\n            if N >= L:  # all chunks are size 1 or less; already at finest granularity\n                break\n            else:\n                N *= 2\n    # after loop, enforce one-minimal:\n    # try removing each single item; if removal possible, do it and continue\n    changed = True\n    while changed:\n        changed = False\n        for i, item in enumerate(current):\n            cand = current[:i] + current[i+1:]\n            attempts += 1\n            if interesting(cand):\n                current = cand\n                changed = True\n                break\n    # verify one-minimal\n    one_minimal = True\n    for i, item in enumerate(current):\n        cand = current[:i] + current[i+1:]\n        if interesting(cand):\n            one_minimal = False\n            break\n    # return\n    return {\n        \"reduced_items\": current,\n        \"attempts\": attempts,\n        \"one_minimal\": one_minimal\n    }\n\nBut note: The spec says \"explicit one-minimal check\" – likely means we should include that check in the output, not necessarily that we must enforce it. However, it's safer to ensure the result is one-minimal (by the final loop), then report that it is minimal.\n\nBut the description of the algorithm may already guarantee one-minimality. However, we can implement the final check and adjust accordingly.\n\nAlso note: \"Do not mutate case\" – we are copying.\n\nNow, I need to output the code as a JSON string. I'll write the Python source as a multiline string and escape. I must ensure no trailing whitespace, proper indentation.\n\nLet me produce the final JSON."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":814,"completion_tokens":6051,"total_tokens":6865,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":512},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":5627},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":512,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":302},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}